---
title: "Installation"
description: "Install the QQL CLI and the qql-rs SDKs for Rust, Python, Node.js, and WebAssembly."
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

## Prerequisites

QQL targets Qdrant 1.19.0 or newer. Pin the server version when running it
in Docker:

{% terminal title="Qdrant" %}
docker run --rm -p 6333:6333 -p 6334:6334 qdrant/qdrant:v1.19.0
{% /terminal %}

Text embedding (any `USING` step) needs an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. With
Ollama, start the server and pull the model before configuring QQL:

{% terminal title="Ollama" %}
ollama serve
ollama pull all-minilm:l6-v2
{% /terminal %}

Then point QQL at both services (see Quickstart). Literal-vector queries skip
the embedder entirely.

## CLI

{% terminal title="Linux and macOS" %}
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/srimon12/qql-rs/main/scripts/install.sh | sh
{% /terminal %}

{% terminal title="Windows PowerShell" %}
irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/srimon12/qql-rs/main/scripts/install.ps1 | iex
{% /terminal %}

The installed binary is `qql`. Run `qql setup` to configure your Qdrant
connection and embedding defaults, then run `qql doctor` to verify connectivity.

{% terminal title="Configure and verify the CLI" %}
qql version
qql setup
qql doctor
{% /terminal %}

{% aside type="note" title="Editors" %}
Syntax highlighting, live diagnostics, and autocompletion are available from
the `qql-lang` VS Code extension (see [Editors](/docs/tools/editors/)).
{% /aside %}

## SDKs

{% grid style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%,18rem), 1fr)); gap:1rem;" %}

{% glassCard title="Rust" %}
{% terminal title="Cargo" %}
cargo add qql qql-core
{% /terminal %}
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="Python" %}
{% terminal title="pip" %}
pip install pyqql
{% /terminal %}
Use `pyqql-edge` only when the in-process edge backend is required.
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="Node.js" %}
{% terminal title="npm" %}
npm install @veristamp/nqql
{% /terminal %}
Use `@veristamp/nqql-edge` for the heavier edge-enabled build.
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="WebAssembly" %}
{% terminal title="npm" %}
npm install qql-wasm
{% /terminal %}
Initialize the module before calling browser exports.
{% /glassCard %}

{% /grid %}

## Build from source

{% terminal title="Workspace build" %}
git clone https://github.com/srimon12/qql-rs.git
cd qql-rs
cargo build --release --workspace
{% /terminal %}

### Feature builds (lean by default)

The default binary — including the prebuilt archives fetched by the
installer above — is `rest` + `grpc` only. `edge` (ONNX/FastEmbed, heavy)
and `record` (axum proxy, cheap) stay opt-in so the default download stays
small. Install a feature build from crates.io with one command (no clone):

{% terminal title="Feature installs (no clone needed)" %}
cargo install qql-cli --locked --features edge
cargo install qql-cli --locked --features record
cargo install qql-cli --locked --features full
{% /terminal %}

Run `qql version` to confirm: its `features` array lists what the binary
supports. Python/Node stay split the same way — `pyqql` /
`@veristamp/nqql` are lean, `pyqql-edge` / `@veristamp/nqql-edge` (plus
`uv pip install pyqql-edge` / `uv add pyqql-edge`) carry the edge runtime.

### Edge runtime

The in-process edge backend is an opt-in build. Compile the CLI with the `edge`
feature to enable `--edge` and `qql config edge`:

{% terminal title="Edge-enabled CLI build" %}
cargo install qql-cli --locked --features edge
# ... or from a local checkout:
# cargo build --release -p qql-cli --features edge
{% /terminal %}

See [the edge backend overview](/docs/edge/) for capabilities and limits.

The runtime defaults to REST and gRPC. Edge and embedded model support are
opt-in because they add substantial binary and model weight.
